e mystery, which was pr"],[19058003,1,["Jerome"],["Jerome: Against the Pelagians"]],[45005014,1,["Jerome"],["Jerome: Against the Pelagians"]],[23066007,1,["John Damascus"],["John Damascus: Exposition of the Orthodox Faith"]],[23007014,1,["Rufinus"],["Rufinus: Commentary on the Apostles' Creed"]]],"f":[["Cassian",2,["On the Incarnation of the Lord, Against Nestorius"],"Ambrose, that illustrious priest of God, who never leaving the Lord's hand, ever shone like a jewel upon the finger of God, thus speaks in his book to the Virgins: \"My brother is white and ruddy.  White because He is the glory of the Father: ruddy because He was born of the Virgi"],["Jerome",2,["Against the Pelagians","Letters"],"Ps. lviii. 3. Sinners are estranged from the womb; that is, either, as St. Paul says (Rom. v. 14), they sin \"after the similitude of Adam\"; or, \"when Christ, as the firstborn, opened the virgin's womb\" (Exod. xiii. 2). The heretics refused to acknowledge the mystery, which was pr"],["Clement Alexandria",1,["The Stromata"],"Now such to us are the Scriptures of the Lord, which gave birth to the truth and continue virgin, in the concealment of the mysteries of the truth. \"And she brought forth, and yet brought not forth,\"  says the Scripture; as having conceived of herself, and not from conjunction. W"],["John Damascus",1,["Exposition of the Orthodox Faith"]],["Methodius",1,["Oration Concerning Simeon and Anna"]],["Rufinus",1,["Commentary on the Apostles' Creed"]]]},"3":{"l":[[42014001,8.6,"c",["G740","G5315","G758"]],[40007013,7.4,"c",["G4439","G3598"]],[40007014,7.4,"c",["G4439","G3598"]],[40026017,7.1,"c",["G106","G5315"]],[41014012,7.1,"c",["G106","G5315"]],[41006008,7.0,"c",["G740","G3598"]],[42009003,7.0,"c",["G740","G3598"]],[42024035,7.0,"c",["G740","G3598"]],[1028020,12.2,"s",["H3899","H1870","H398"]],[9028022,12.2,"s",["H3899","H1870","H398"]],[11013009,12.2,"s",["H3899","H1870","H398"]],[11013017,12.2,"s",["H3899","H1870","H398"]],[26040007,11.5,"s",["H197","H81